{"title":"2012 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Vector_projection_overkill)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Vector projection overkill)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/3/8d3b562c2718f735ae9d455f8b1280a128824fc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0 ,0 ,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/3/40351a54433aa452afdfc6195970641d012c2158.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1, 0, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/0/8b00149795d04641995639bade885a65bff6cb2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 2, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49cffcb8ee93e317c6afd392be3a832c3b970a18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0, 3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />. Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> are midpoints of line segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/c/0cc3986abcac5506776beeb9e8009c12069b2646.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BD},\\text{ }  \\overline{AB}, \\text{ } \\overline {AC},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"118\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/b/63b7e5ec2db31e9aded4bb09881b42d1c439fce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{DC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> respectively. What is the area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/3/9735b37ac43861ef9e105bff1711f117917b09ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EFGH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/c/1bc135a8bdfcfba0945a9711466eb37b9c7b9bbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\sqrt{2}\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\sqrt{3}\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"462\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider a tetrahedron with vertices at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/5/8859dbfb7a9ec734e16f2a88fb5cf1104eb6a39b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B,C,D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" /> in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/7/d57d8fef9329fabd73078ae824a034e8a00e332f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xyz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"11\" />-space. The length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is just one-half of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> because it is the midsegment of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/94585e5639fd985bbe71e9039a8681af8e09d680.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD.$\" width=\"63\" height=\"13\" /> The same concept applies to the other side lengths. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/8/6e867467073fae9550c977a21ee2cb19426c4f92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD=3$\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/e/69ea4bf2b461449a56d9291685e14b2240a423cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2}=\\sqrt{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"174\" height=\"21\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/1/7a1166ed355a00212e8fecdea367ea576ebfc1c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF=HG=\\frac32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"119\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/1/d51ed7a7f9bfc1381437c2a66b2faa21b74ed317.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EH=FG=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"134\" height=\"41\" />. The line segments lie on perpendicular planes so quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/3/9735b37ac43861ef9e105bff1711f117917b09ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EFGH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" /> is a rectangle. The area is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/c/0ac93aa0c3d28811407d96e8211f079322b5d869.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[EF \\cdot FG = \\frac 32 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt 5} 4\\implies \\boxed{\\textbf C}.\\]\" width=\"288\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/3/e93d5efaf2b0c198ac89b154f643a4524951d4ed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import three; draw((0,0,0)--(1,0,0)--(0,0,3)--cycle); draw((0,0,0)--(0,2,0)); draw((0,2,0)--(0,0,3)); //EFGH draw((0.5,0,1.5)--(0.5,0,0)--(0,1,0)--(0,1,1.5)--(0.5,0,1.5),red); //Points label(&quot;$E$&quot;,(0.5,0,1.5),NW); label(&quot;$F$&quot;,(0.5,0,0),S); label(&quot;$G$&quot;,(0,1,0),S); label(&quot;$H$&quot;,(0,1,1.5),NE); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,(0,0,0),NE); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,(1,0,0),S); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,(0,2,0),S); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,(0,0,3),N); [/asy]\" width=\"155\" height=\"242\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Computing the points of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/3/9735b37ac43861ef9e105bff1711f117917b09ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EFGH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/a/4da86b672229a20c5a681da17a35c8cb6d8ec6a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(0.5, 0, 1.5), F(0.5, 0, 0), G(0,1,0), H(0,1,1.5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"369\" height=\"18\" />.  The vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/5/305e230c3081a51a672ce9506092b3b5a1d735ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,-1.5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />, while the vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/b/68bc6f8caca948dca03f953aee8ce093afad4d88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/5/305e230c3081a51a672ce9506092b3b5a1d735ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,-1.5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />, meaning the two sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/7/0a7a248cfd02095e2a009f811d5732a9bea9d595.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> are parallel.  Similarly, the vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/3951902c11a1cd39a091ea15f0f06cbc79095de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/4/65454cea29d3279ea9b214384bb8888229093084.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-0.5, 1, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />, while the vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/b/90b19c0bfdec36082e8da6e7ba3a3149b69fa63c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/4/65454cea29d3279ea9b214384bb8888229093084.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-0.5, 1, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />.  Again, these are equal in both magnitude and direction, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/3951902c11a1cd39a091ea15f0f06cbc79095de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/b/90b19c0bfdec36082e8da6e7ba3a3149b69fa63c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> are parallel.  Thus, figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/3/9735b37ac43861ef9e105bff1711f117917b09ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EFGH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" /> is a parallelogram.\n</p><p>Computation of vectors <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/b/68bc6f8caca948dca03f953aee8ce093afad4d88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> is sufficient evidence that the figure is a parallelogram, since the vectors are not only point in the same direction, but are of the same magnitude, but the other vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/3951902c11a1cd39a091ea15f0f06cbc79095de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is needed to find the angle between the sides.\n</p><p>Taking the dot product of vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> and vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/3951902c11a1cd39a091ea15f0f06cbc79095de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/3/a93b5b54dc4931ec3cfd145c233d1dd0ff93b0ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\cdot -0.5 + 0 \\cdot 1 + -1.5 \\cdot 0 = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"230\" height=\"15\" />, which means the two vectors are perpendicular.  (Alternately, as above, note that vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/2/c128243ea8125d0bb007b8132dcfa44461df73a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> goes directly down on the z-axis, while vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/3951902c11a1cd39a091ea15f0f06cbc79095de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> has no z-component and lie completely in the xy plane.)  Thus, the figure is a parallelogram with a right angle, which makes it a rectangle.  With the distance formula in three dimensions, we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/e/5ae63c4c7f1be0a8afef79a155aab508dc600042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF = \\frac{3}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"64\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/0/c208bcd21ea70067eaa1a334befaf0f00fa61349.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FG = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"79\" height=\"41\" />, giving an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a45eeff3a07782cc6a1eb90a23546ccf789ab816.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac32 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"163\" height=\"54\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Vector_projection_overkill.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Vector_projection_overkill)\">Solution 3 (Vector projection overkill)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Vector projection overkill)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using the midpoint formula, we obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/7/a9751d644709cd5e87b354fcdf65c513756c98bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E=\\left(\\frac12,0,\\frac32\\right),\\quad F=\\left(\\frac12,0,0\\right),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"266\" height=\"45\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/2/2c2f071c1d5c0a46a99b9ffa97bcd563de23eedf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G=(0,1,0),\\quad H=\\left(0,1,\\frac32\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"245\" height=\"45\" />\nConsider two adjacent vectors:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/4/c84eb4517379aafef88a9c84097097371545fef9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{FE}=\\left(0,0,\\frac32\\right),\\qquad \\overrightarrow{FG}=\\left(-\\frac12,1,0\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"321\" height=\"45\" /> Their cross product gives a normal vector to the plane containing the quadrilateral: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/9/6e95cecbd20348655cddcd4b66a11b58130567ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf n=\\overrightarrow{FE}\\times\\overrightarrow{FG}=\\left(-\\frac32,-\\frac34,0\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"255\" height=\"45\" />\nMultiplying by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/b/fab8b88151611f7798fc64ffbed983f727b247a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-4/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" />, we obtain the simpler normal vector <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/3/d63ed178af6bfe79a068e40734a2a6a6a818f37f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />. Thus, the plane containing the quadrilateral has equation \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/0/440d376c23c6d9ee2e868e7c23dbfa6e52852bd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x+y=1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p>Since the normal vector has a zero <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />-component, we project the quadrilateral orthogonally onto the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/3/5d379b13c2338ee58a27252096b2544c9e8e37c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$yz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"11\" />-plane by dropping the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-coordinate. The projected vertices, in order, are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/9/8699f3d217aca1ea1aa8871f2a5420416e306a8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E&#39;=\\left(0,\\frac32\\right),\\quad F&#39;=(0,0),\\quad G&#39;=(1,0),\\quad H&#39;=\\left(1,\\frac32\\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"456\" height=\"45\" />\nApplying the shoelace formula,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/5/8c5c5261f8c174f6b0bde713c561475b81f481eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{aligned} A_{\\text{proj}} &amp;=\\frac12\\left| 0+0+\\frac32+\\frac32-0 \\right|\\\\ &amp;=\\frac32. \\end{aligned}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -39px\" width=\"235\" height=\"88\" />\n</p><p>However, orthogonal projection changes the area by the cosine of the angle between the two planes.\nThe normal vectors to the original plane and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/3/5d379b13c2338ee58a27252096b2544c9e8e37c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$yz$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"11\" />-plane are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/3/d63ed178af6bfe79a068e40734a2a6a6a818f37f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/0/d10e4a7cc979f7d958177f48e71c33f4f908c116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" />, respectively.\nTherefore,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/8/8f8ca7fc3e7db62b1857958a43e7b23e8a203170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos\\theta =\\frac{|(2,1,0)\\cdot(1,0,0)|} {\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}} =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"262\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Hence the actual area is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/a/2bab0b1b110e680dc715a25bc8ffef7b6cf53fd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=\\frac{A_{\\text{proj}}}{\\cos\\theta} =\\frac{\\frac32}{\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}} =\\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt5}{4}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"210\" height=\"58\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/f/fffc7368e8e7b77c88b0b4bf732c88c60e27b439.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"42\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Sunsine\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/videos/amc/2012amc10a/249\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/videos/amc/2012amc10a/249</a>\n</p><p>~dolphin7\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A\">2012 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2012 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel